WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:13.030
Spring is, according to many a poem, a season
of awakening.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:20.320
The dreariness of winter fades and the snow
melts away to reveal green earth.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.830
Animals emerge from their slumber to fill
the landscape with their newly birthed young.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.880
And in our most idyllic imaginings, the air
carries the sound of chirping birds and the

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:37.850
wondrous scents of daffodils, and lilacs,
and other fragrant flowers.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.050
But nature is a messy business.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.980
Spring isn't just sunshine and greenery, it
is also rain and mud.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:54.190
And sometimes, puncturing through that floral
perfume are some sour notes.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.600
Maybe you've wandered near a pond and caught
a strange hint of cucumber.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.860
Or maybe that smell has deepened into something
more rancid, like cod liver oil.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:12.320
If you've caught this unpleasant aroma, you
might be experiencing spring in the microcosmos.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:23.520
As the weather gets warmer, lakes and ponds
begin to thaw, affecting even the tiniest residents.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.710
The water around them is still cool, but it
turns out that's perfect.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:33.740
Because when spring comes, flowers aren't
the only thing that bloom.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.920
Synura are found in freshwater throughout
the year, joining together in varied numbers

00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:47.990
to create spherical colonies.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.359
There are around 50 species that are currently
recognized from around the world, including

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:54.869
in the Arctic.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:02:00.529
The only continent they have not been found on yet is Antarctica, but, I mean, who can blame them?

00:02:00.529 --> 00:02:07.539
These varied species of Synura are distinguished
in part by the structure of the complex silica

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:09.700
scales that adorn them.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:15.360
We can't see those scales with our microscopes,
but scientists with electron microscopes are

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.980
able to analyze and categorize those structures.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:27.310
The scales are formed inside specified vesicles
and then molded into shape by structures on

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:35.370
the endoplasmic reticulum and then they are arranged into overlapping rows along the cell surface.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:42.220
But for all the order and hardness implied
by knowledge of this scaly exterior, if you

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:49.001
take a wider view, synura kind of look like
marigolds tumbling around in the wind, like

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:51.610
flowery confetti.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:57.870
Each individual cell contains two flagella,
and when they form a colony, those flagella

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:05.320
are pointed outward, and the movement of the flagella, of course, is what drives that jumbled swimming.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.900
Aside from their petal-like appearance, Synura
also resemble marigolds because of their distinctive

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.840
yellow color.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.510
These organisms are chrysophytes, also known
as golden algae.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:27.290
And on the first hearing, "golden algae" might
sound a bit strange.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:32.490
Algae are photosynthetic, and most of the
ones we've met in the microcosmos are green

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.480
because of the pigmented chlorophyll inside
of their chloroplasts.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:45.260
Well, each Synura cell has two chloroplasts, just two, and those chloroplasts do contain chlorophyll.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:51.290
But they're also colored with xanthophyll,
a yellow-brown accessory pigment that helps

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.470
the organism process light at other wavelengths
for photosynthesis.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:01.940
The xanthophyll ends up overshadowing the
green color of the chlorophyll, and as the

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.850
colonies of synura begin to accumulate in
a bloom, you might see the water they inhabit

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.240
become brownish in color as well.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:24.560
Synura bloom in autumn as well as spring, but they're still present at low numbers throughout the year.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.940
And even when their populations are more modest,
their scent might still be apparent.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:39.210
It only takes about 5-10 colonies in a milliliter
of water to produce a cucumber smell.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:44.590
So imagine what it's like to be around a pond
where the bloom is in full swing.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:50.250
Even a single drop of water might hold tens
of thousands of these colonies.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.670
It's fortunate maybe then that smell-o-vision
never took.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:05:03.120
If it had, we would definitely be confronting you right now with a very unpleasant fish odor.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.940
When synura bloom, the water around them ends
up filled with a cocktail of alkenes, aldehydes,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.380
alcohols, and ketones that are likely the
product of their metabolism.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.900
As the colonies accumulate, so too do these
compounds.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.720
And it's not like all of these chemicals have
a particularly bad aroma.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:28.990
Synura have been associated with the release
of beta-ionone, which smells like violets,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.100
and beta-cyclocitral, which smells like tobacco.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:38.690
These sound like the fragrance notes you might
read on a nice, expensive bottle of perfume.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.080
But of course, that's not all that's in the
water.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:50.320
Synura also produces trans,cis-deca-2,4-dienal, a compound whose name probably means nothing

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:56.840
to you until you happen upon its rancid smell
overshadowing all of the other aromas.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:02.520
And while this may not be the most welcome
whiff when you are walking near a pond, the

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.090
smell is handy if you happen to be microbe
hunting.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:12.230
So much of sampling is a mysterious, subtle
business, whether you're running a plankton

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.330
net through a lake or sticking your arm in
a mall aquarium tank.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:22.650
You're searching for the invisible, which
means that you have to use environmental cues

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.940
to figure out what you might be collecting.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.270
And you usually will not know what microbes
you’ve gathered until later when you get

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.270
them under the microscope.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:38.960
But as the wise Gandalf once said, "If in
doubt, Meriadoc, always follow your nose"

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:44.970
And the smell of synura may not be pleasant,
but it is honest.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:51.490
If you want to observe many, many synura,
then you can track the smell to its bloom.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:56.620
But with blooms like this, it can be difficult
to find other organisms in your sample, so

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.440
you might end up using the smell as more of
a warning to stay away.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:09.280
Synura, of course, are not the only microorganisms
to multiply into blooms.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.350
And just as you can't understand the entirety of the microcosmos through any one microbe,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:19.389
you cannot understand algal blooms by studying
just one algae.

00:07:19.389 --> 00:07:25.120
Their causes can vary from seasonal changes
to water pollution, and their effects can

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.890
range as well.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:32.130
Some blooms are not harmful, while others
can be toxic to other organisms and damage

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.160
local ecosystems.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:41.270
But at their core, algal blooms demonstrate
the ingenuity of microbes, their talent for

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:46.740
expansion, turning even the tiniest organisms
on our planet into a presence

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.280
that none can ignore.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:57.169
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:02.130
